# Adult Literacy as a Workforce Readiness Indicator
## A Case Study of San Antonio and Bexar County, Texas

**Project Collaborators:** Kathleen R. Cassell; 
**Project Stage:** Research Scoping and Data Discovery  
**Target Research Period:** 2020–2026  
**Tentative Completion Date:** December 31, 2026  

## Project Overview

This collaborative data analytics project examines available adult literacy evidence alongside demographic, educational, and workforce indicators in San Antonio and Bexar County, Texas.

The study aims to identify patterns that may inform adult education referrals, workforce development planning, and future research. Publicly available data will support interactive dashboards and written analysis, with documented sources, methods, and limitations.

### Primary Research Question

> What does available adult literacy evidence reveal about workforce readiness and educational accessibility in San Antonio and Bexar County, and what opportunities warrant further investigation or targeted support?

## Study Scope

| Dimension | Scope |
| --- | --- |
| Geographic coverage | San Antonio and Bexar County, reported separately where possible. Texas and national comparisons will be included where measures are comparable. |
| Research period | Target period of 2020–2026, subject to availability. Data reference periods and publication dates will be documented separately. |
| Target population | Adults ages 18–45. Sources using other age ranges will be explicitly labeled and assessed for suitability. |
| Literacy evidence | Definitions, assessment methods, available measures, reported estimates, and supported comparisons. |
| Contextual indicators | Educational attainment, income, employment, labor force participation, language spoken at home, and English proficiency. |
| Education resources | Publicly documented adult literacy, ESL, adult basic education, library, community college, and workforce education programs. |
| Access considerations | Program locations, eligibility, cost, delivery format, and schedules where documented. |
| Analytical approach | Descriptive analysis, geographic comparisons, and exploratory association analysis where supported by compatible data. |

### Analytical Boundaries

- Distinguish direct literacy measures from modeled estimates and contextual indicators.
- Do not treat educational attainment or English proficiency as substitutes for measured literacy.
- Do not infer individual characteristics from community-level patterns.
- Do not interpret associations as evidence of causation.
- Do not treat program proximity alone as proof of accessibility.
- Do not generate unsupported annual literacy estimates or individual literacy scores.

Interviews, surveys, collection of personal information, and program effectiveness evaluations are outside the initial scope.

